{"candidates":[{"claim":"A panel of non-experts using a checklist will be more reliable for tasks where the underlying causal structure is sparse and additive, because the checklist enforces coverage of sparse sufficient cues; however, for tasks with dense, configural causal interactions, the expert's intuitive high-dimensional matching dominates.","counterconditions":["If the checklist includes conditional logic (IF A AND B THEN C) that fully captures the configural interactions, the panel's reliability gain extends to dense causal structures.","If the expert's intuition is actually linear and heuristic (low-dimensional), the expert may match or exceed the checklist even on sparse structures due to faster, more calibrated retrieval."],"mechanism":"Checklists linearize the evidence-integration process, enforcing coverage of individually necessary or sufficient cues. Non-expert panels aggregate this linearized evidence by averaging independent sampling errors. Expert intuition, however, operates as a high-dimensional pattern matcher that implicitly configures cue interactions. For sparse, additive task structures, the lossy compression of intuition is penalized (it overlays unnecessary interactions), while the checklist's lossless encoding of the subset wins. For configural tasks, the checklist's failure to encode interactions dooms it.","optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_005","SCR_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_006","SRC_007"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Within a single evaluation, a genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere checklist compliance by perturbing a single necessary cue: a genuine panel will degrade gracefully (tracking the causal necessity), while mere compliance will produce dense, undifferentiated errors across both necessary and merely-correlated cues.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is entirely deterministic and the panel follows it perfectly, perturbation yields deterministic errors but does not prove structural understanding.","If the expert's intuition is similarly relying on the same surface correlations, the expert will show the same dense error pattern, erasing the distinction."],"mechanism":"If the panel encodes the checklist as a structural model (genuine reliability), perturbing a necessary cue breaks the causal chain and yields errors specifically on tasks where that cue was necessary. If the panel is using the checklist as a surface heuristic (mere compliance), perturbation breaks the superficial correlation, leading to errors on a broader, less structured set of tasks. The error-density signature on perturbation distinguishes structural understanding from surface matching.","optional_refs":["SCR_003","SRC_006","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is real only if it survives a transfer test to held-out tasks where the checklist's items are permuted or re-described; if the gain evaporates, the panel was relying on lexical or order-specific compliance rather than structural understanding.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is effectively a rigid decision tree, permuting it destroys valid performance even if the panel genuinely understood the structure.","If the expert's intuition is similarly lexicalized or order-dependent, the expert will also fail the transfer test."],"mechanism":"Checklist compliance is often keyed to surface features of the checklist itself (item order, phrasing). A genuine reliability gain implies the panel has extracted the underlying cues and can re-apply them. Permuting or re-describing items tests whether the panel's reliability is attached to the checklist's surface or its structure.","optional_refs":["SCR_002","SRC_008","SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"The panel advantage is a statistical artifact of averaging independent errors and vanishes when the checklist itself is a high-variance, ambiguous instrument; the expert dominates whenever the task requires resolving checklist ambiguity.","counterconditions":["If the panel is trained to resolve ambiguity using a shared protocol, the correlated-error collapse may be mitigated.","If the expert's intuition is itself biased by the same ambiguities, the expert may not dominate."],"mechanism":"A panel of non-experts using a checklist reduces variance by averaging independent errors. But this gain is conditional on the checklist being unambiguous. When the checklist is ambiguous or requires judgment, the non-experts' independent errors become correlated (they all misread the ambiguity in the same way), collapsing the variance-reduction benefit. The expert's intuition, trained on resolving ambiguity, dominates.","optional_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"claim":"Mere checklist compliance is distinguished from genuine reliability gain by testing whether the panel's errors, when they occur, are clustered on tasks requiring cue integration (where the checklist fails to specify the combination) versus spread across single-cue tasks (where compliance is sufficient).","counterconditions":["If the checklist fully specifies cue integration, the panel may not error on integration tasks, but this does not prove genuine understanding.","If the expert's intuition also fails on integration tasks, the error-location signature does not distinguish panel from expert."],"mechanism":"If the panel is genuinely reliable, its errors should be confined to the boundary of the checklist's coverage—tasks requiring integration of cues that the checklist does not specify. If the panel is merely compliant, its errors will be spread across single-cue tasks where the checklist was sufficient but the panel failed to apply it. The error-location signature distinguishes structural understanding from surface compliance.","optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"A panel of non-experts using a checklist will be more reliable than a single expert only when the task has a low base rate of the outcome, because the checklist enforces base-rate awareness; the expert's intuition is biased by salient cues and ignores base rates.","counterconditions":["If the expert is trained in Bayesian reasoning, the base-rate bias may be mitigated.","If the checklist does not include base-rate items, the panel will share the expert's bias."],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is subject to representativeness and availability biases, which overweight salient cues and underweight base rates. A checklist explicitly forces the panel to attend to base-rate information. For low-base-rate tasks, this forced calibration yields a reliability gain. For high-base-rate tasks, the expert's intuition is sufficient.","optional_refs":["SRC_003"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}]}